{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CONFIRMED, with measurements — this upgrades the earlier \"predicted escape\" comment on this epic from\na prediction to an observed instance, and the prediction's own disconfirming test did NOT fire.\n\nEarlier I argued this epic's framing was drawn in the wrong place: not REACHABILITY of ground truth, but\nCITATION of it. I logged a candidate story and an explicit way to disprove it (\"if the review DOES surface\nthe routing gap unprompted, drop the candidate\"). It did not. Here is the evidence.\n\nTHE ESCAPE: the per-pass model collapse — one env var cannot express four passes; `verify.py:269` returns\nthe verifier default ONLY when the model equals the framework default, so ANY non-default model (every\nBedrock id) silently disables the Pass-2/Pass-4 downgrade; and Pass-1's ladder is BARE `claude-*` names\nthat infer provider `anthropic`, so a \"Bedrock\" run can still send its finders to direct Anthropic. Now\ntracked as 3768-789c-11ed-48be, which blocks BOTH remaining stories of epic 061c.\n\nMEASUREMENT 1 — the gate never ran on the two tickets where the defect lives. Counting REVIEW_RESULT\nevents in the store: epic 061c has 2 (it WAS reviewed, and signed). Story eb6e (S8, the cutover) has\nZERO. Story 0d76 (S6, the parity bar) has ZERO. Plan-review runs at CLAIM time, and neither story has\nbeen claimed. So for those two, \"the review missed it\" is FALSE — no review has ever run. That distinction\nmatters for this epic's design: a criterion that only fires at claim time cannot protect a decomposition.\n\nMEASUREMENT 2 — nothing pointed at the defect. I checked whether ANY ticket in the tree (the epic, S5\n343b, S6 0d76, S8 eb6e) names `resolve_verifier_model`, `verify.py`, or `model_ladder` anywhere in its\ndescription. The answer is 0 of 3 for EVERY one of them. The reviewer reads the material the plan cites;\nno plan cited the three files where the collapse lives. Ground truth was 100% in-repo, needed no AWS call,\nand was a three-hop trace — and was still invisible.\n\nMEASUREMENT 3 — the epic-level review passed and signed. So a reviewed, signed plan sat directly above a\ndefect that made two of its stories unimplementable as written. An epic plan describes the GOAL; the\ndefect lived in the mechanism, which no story's Scope named.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY TRIGGERED THE DISCOVERY: an operator stating a desired end state (\"Pass-1 should use opus,\nPass-2/Pass-4 sonnet\"), which forced the question IS THIS EXPRESSIBLE. No gate asks that. Gates ask\nwhether a plan is well-formed, grounded and testable. S8's \"The model stays Claude; only the route\nchanges\" is well-formed, grounded-sounding, and reads as commendable SCOPE DISCIPLINE — the camouflage is\nthat the defect looks like a virtue.\n\nTHE SAME SHAPE RECURRED TWICE MORE IN THE SAME RUN, which is why I think it is a class and not an anecdote:\n- Story 343b was reviewed, PASSED, implemented under held-out TDD with mutation checks, and still shipped a\n  field that never reaches a production payload — because every test fed the persist step a hand-built\n  verdict, and the uncited assembly path (`orchestrator.finalize_verdict`) was never exercised. Found only\n  by an [operator-attested] criterion demanding a REAL run.\n- 3768's own plan-review then found a THIRD inverted gate (`completion.py:332`) that my plan had not cited\n  — again, the uncited path was the broken one.\n\nREFINED CANDIDATE CRITERION for this epic: advisory-first, fires when a plan asserts UNCHANGED BEHAVIOUR\nacross a substrate/route/provider swap while citing NO file on the path that consumes the swapped\nconfiguration. Detection sketch: a swap-shaped plan (provider/route/endpoint/model change) whose Scope\ntouches only configuration, and whose citations include no consumer of that configuration. The remedy it\nasks for is cheap: cite the resolution chain, or restate the assertion as an open question. S8 would have\ntripped it on \"only the route changes\" with a Scope of one env var.\n\nSECOND, NARROWER CANDIDATE suggested by measurement 1: the decomposition itself is unguarded. Stories are\nreviewed only when claimed, so a tree can be decomposed, signed at the epic level, and sit for a whole\nimplementation run with story-level defects unexamined. Worth considering whether an epic-level review\nshould be able to require that its children's mechanisms are cited somewhere, even before they are claimed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785466225299417001,"uuid":"c11f9a37-1582-42bc-87a0-cbdc65fdfbdb"}